RiceLink — Product Definition
Document: Product DefinitionProduct Name: RiceLinkVersion: 1.0Status: Initial Product SpecificationDate: November 2026

1. Product Overview
RiceLink is a multi-crop agricultural supply-chain coordination platform designed to connect agricultural producers, aggregators, processors, storage providers, logistics operators and buyers.
The platform provides a shared digital environment where agricultural participants can make produce supply visible, communicate procurement requirements, discover relevant commercial opportunities and coordinate the movement of agricultural products from production locations to processing facilities and markets.
RiceLink is designed to support multiple agricultural commodities within one platform, including grains, legumes, roots and tubers, fruits, vegetables and cash crops.
Rather than focusing on a single crop or a single transaction, RiceLink aims to provide coordination infrastructure for the wider agricultural supply chain.
Product Positioning
RiceLink — Connecting agricultural production to markets, processing and distribution.
Core Idea
Agricultural production does not end when a farmer harvests a crop. Produce must move through several participants and processes before reaching its final destination.
RiceLink aims to make these connections more visible, accessible and coordinated.

2. Product Vision
To build a connected agricultural supply-chain ecosystem that improves how agricultural produce moves from farms to markets, processors and consumers.
We envision a future where agricultural producers, businesses and service providers can discover one another, coordinate activities and make better decisions using accessible agricultural supply-chain information.
3. Product Mission
To provide digital infrastructure that connects agricultural supply and demand, simplifies coordination between value-chain participants and supports more efficient movement of agricultural products.
4. The Problem
Agricultural supply chains involve multiple independent participants, including farmers, aggregators, processors, storage operators, transport providers and buyers.
However, coordination between these participants can be difficult.
Some of the challenges RiceLink intends to investigate and address include:
4.1 Fragmented Supply Information
Agricultural produce may be available across different farms, communities and producer groups, making it difficult for buyers to understand where supply exists and how much is available.
4.2 Difficulty Sourcing Agricultural Produce
Processors, distributors and institutional buyers may struggle to identify suitable suppliers who can meet their required quantities, locations, quality specifications and delivery periods.
4.3 Limited Market Visibility
Producers may not have sufficient visibility into potential buyers and procurement opportunities.
4.4 Aggregation Challenges
Large buyers may require quantities that individual farmers cannot independently provide. Coordinating supply from multiple producers can therefore become complicated.
4.5 Storage and Transportation Coordination
The availability of produce does not automatically mean that it can reach its intended destination. Storage capacity, transportation availability, handling requirements and delivery coordination can introduce additional challenges.
4.6 Limited Supply-Chain Information
Participants may have limited access to useful information about agricultural supply, demand, availability and market activity.
Note: These are problem areas the product intends to address. Their prevalence, severity and commercial importance must be validated through research with actual agricultural stakeholders.

5. Target Users and Stakeholders
RiceLink is designed for participants across agricultural value chains.
5.1 Agricultural Producers
Includes:
Individual farmers.
Commercial farms.
Farmer cooperatives.
Producer associations.
Agricultural production groups.
Their needs include:
Making available produce visible.
Finding potential buyers.
Communicating expected harvests.
Understanding procurement demand.
Connecting with aggregators and processors.
5.2 Aggregators
Aggregators source agricultural produce from multiple producers and consolidate quantities to meet buyer requirements.
Their needs include:
Discovering available produce.
Identifying producers and farming communities.
Coordinating supply quantities.
Consolidating agricultural products.
Finding potential buyers.
Managing supply opportunities.
5.3 Processors
Processors convert agricultural raw materials into finished or semi-finished products.
Examples include:
Rice mills.
Cassava processing companies.
Maize processors.
Soybean processors.
Food manufacturing businesses.
Their needs include:
Communicating procurement requirements.
Finding reliable suppliers.
Identifying available quantities.
Comparing supply opportunities.
Coordinating procurement.
5.4 Storage Providers
Includes:
Warehouses.
Agricultural storage facilities.
Cold-storage operators.
Grain storage facilities.
Their needs include:
Making storage services visible.
Communicating available capacity.
Connecting with agricultural businesses requiring storage.
Coordinating storage enquiries.
5.5 Logistics Operators
Includes:
Truck owners.
Agricultural transport companies.
Independent logistics providers.
Distribution operators.
Their needs include:
Discovering transport requests.
Communicating service coverage.
Connecting with producers, aggregators and buyers.
Coordinating produce transportation.
5.6 Buyers
Includes:
Commodity traders.
Food distributors.
Retailers.
Wholesale buyers.
Institutional buyers.
Export-oriented businesses.
Their needs include:
Finding agricultural products.
Publishing procurement requirements.
Discovering available supply.
Connecting with suitable suppliers.
Coordinating sourcing and delivery.

6. Product Objectives
RiceLink aims to:
Connect agricultural producers with relevant market participants.
Improve visibility of agricultural supply and procurement demand.
Support multiple crops within one platform.
Make agricultural sourcing and procurement more organised.
Support aggregation and coordination of produce from multiple sources.
Improve discoverability of storage and logistics services.
Support better coordination across agricultural value chains.
Generate useful information about agricultural supply and demand.
Build a scalable digital foundation for agricultural commerce and coordination.

7. Product Modules
The platform will be organised around interconnected modules.
7.1 User and Organisation Management
Handles:
User registration.
Authentication.
User profiles.
Organisation profiles.
Organisation memberships.
Participant roles.
Account verification.
7.2 Agricultural Catalogue
Provides a central catalogue of agricultural products supported by RiceLink.
Crop categories may include:
Grains
Rice.
Maize.
Sorghum.
Millet.
Legumes
Soybeans.
Cowpeas.
Groundnuts.
Roots and Tubers
Cassava.
Yam.
Potatoes.
Fruits and Vegetables
Tomatoes.
Pepper.
Onions.
Plantain.
Cash Crops
Cocoa.
Cashew.
Sesame.
The catalogue must be configurable so additional crops can be introduced without redesigning the platform.
7.3 Agricultural Supply Management
Allows producers and authorised aggregators to publish agricultural supply.
Information may include:
Crop.
Quantity.
Unit of measurement.
Location.
Expected harvest date.
Availability period.
Crop variety.
Quality specifications.
Packaging information.
Asking price, where applicable.
Transportation requirements.
Images and descriptions.
7.4 Procurement and Demand Management
Allows buyers, processors and aggregators to communicate their sourcing requirements.
Information may include:
Required crop.
Required quantity.
Unit of measurement.
Required quality.
Preferred variety.
Delivery location.
Required delivery period.
Packaging requirements.
Price expectations, where applicable.
7.5 Discovery and Matching
Helps participants discover potentially relevant supply and demand.
Matching considerations:
Crop compatibility.
Quantity compatibility.
Location.
Availability period.
Quality requirements.
Variety requirements.
Matches are recommendations for further engagement, not guaranteed transactions.
7.6 Aggregation Management
Supports the coordination of agricultural products sourced from multiple producers.
Potential capabilities:
Aggregation requests.
Source producer records.
Consolidated supply quantities.
Aggregation lot management.
Supply allocation.
Buyer fulfilment coordination.
7.7 Storage Coordination
Allows storage providers to publish their services and enables participants to discover storage opportunities.
Potential information:
Facility location.
Storage type.
Capacity.
Supported commodities.
Availability.
Storage conditions.
Service requirements.
7.8 Logistics Coordination
Connects agricultural participants with transport service providers.
Potential information:
Pickup location.
Destination.
Produce quantity.
Required vehicle or load capacity.
Pickup date.
Delivery date.
Transportation status.
7.9 Enquiry and Communication
Provides a coordination channel between interested participants.
Capabilities:
Initiating enquiries.
Responding to enquiries.
Tracking enquiry status.
Linking enquiries to supply or procurement records.
Managing communication history.
7.10 Analytics and Market Intelligence
Provides information about activity across the platform.
Potential insights:
Supply availability by crop.
Demand by crop.
Supply by location.
Procurement activity.
Agricultural product movement.
Aggregation activity.
Storage and logistics demand.
Advanced market intelligence will depend on the availability and quality of reliable data.

8. Core User Journeys
Producer Journey
Register on RiceLink.
Create a producer profile.
Select the relevant agricultural crop.
Publish available or expected produce.
Specify quantity, unit, location and availability.
Receive enquiries from interested participants.
Communicate with buyers or aggregators.
Update the supply listing when its availability changes.
Buyer Journey
Register on RiceLink.
Create a buyer or organisation profile.
Publish a procurement request.
Specify crop, quantity, quality and delivery requirements.
Discover potentially suitable supply.
Contact producers or aggregators.
Coordinate procurement.
Aggregator Journey
Register as an aggregator.
Create an aggregator profile.
Discover available produce.
Contact producers.
Coordinate supply from multiple sources.
Consolidate produce into an aggregation lot.
Connect the consolidated supply with buyers.
Storage Provider Journey
Register as a storage provider.
Create a facility profile.
Publish storage services and declared capacity.
Receive storage enquiries.
Coordinate storage arrangements.
Logistics Provider Journey
Register as a logistics operator.
Create a service profile.
Specify service locations and transportation capabilities.
Discover transportation requests.
Respond to enquiries.
Coordinate delivery.

