10. Product Principles
Multi-Crop by Design
RiceLink must support multiple crops within a shared system.
Multi-Stakeholder by Design
The platform must support the different responsibilities and needs of agricultural value-chain participants.
Coordination Before Infrastructure Ownership
RiceLink should initially coordinate access to existing storage, transportation and processing services rather than assuming it owns these physical assets.
Data-Driven Development
Product decisions should be informed by agricultural research, stakeholder feedback and actual platform activity.
Trust and Transparency
Users should understand whether information is self-reported, verified or system-generated.
Simplicity
The initial platform should solve essential coordination problems before introducing unnecessary complexity.
Scalability
The platform should accommodate additional crops, regions, organisations and services without requiring fundamental architectural redesign.

11. Business Model Hypotheses
Potential revenue streams include:
Enterprise subscriptions for processors, aggregators and commercial buyers.
Transaction facilitation fees.
Logistics coordination fees.
Storage-service coordination or referral fees.
Premium procurement and supply-management tools.
Agricultural market intelligence subscriptions.
Institutional and agricultural partnerships.
These models must be validated through customer interviews, willingness-to-pay testing and operational experiments.

12. Geographic Strategy
Initial Market
Ibadan, Oyo State, Nigeria.
Expansion
Selected agricultural clusters in Southwest Nigeria.
Other agricultural regions in Nigeria.
Selected African markets.
Expansion should be based on demonstrated adoption, operational capacity and validated market demand.

13. Success Metrics
RiceLink should measure:
Registered producers.
Active producers.
Registered buyers and processors.
Active supply listings.
Active procurement requests.
Number of supported crops with actual activity.
Number of enquiries.
Number of potential matches.
Number of confirmed commercial agreements.
Quantity of produce coordinated.
Number of repeat participants.
Average enquiry response time.
User satisfaction.
Reported improvements in coordination.
A match, listing or enquiry must not be counted as a completed transaction.

14. Product Direction
RiceLink is not intended to remain a simple agricultural marketplace.
Its broader ambition is to become digital coordination infrastructure for agricultural supply chains.
The platform will begin by improving visibility and connections between participants, then progressively support aggregation, storage, transportation, processing and distribution coordination.
The guiding strategy is to start with coordination, establish value and expand into more complex agricultural infrastructure where evidence justifies it.
